.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trk
is a command\-line tool for tracking what you are doing, and why, while your
work branches into side quests. It targets rabbit holes: you start a task,
discover you need another one, then another, and three hours later you cannot
remember why you came here.
.B trk
keeps the chain.
.PP
Most task trackers assume you know, up front, the complete list of steps a job
requires. Real work does not behave that way. You begin with a goal, you take
one step, that step reveals a dependency, the dependency reveals a question,
and the question sends you down a hole. By the time you climb out you have
forgotten the shape of the thing you were originally trying to do.
.B trk
is built for exactly that moment. It treats work as a tree of tasks under a
goal, keeps a pointer to the one task you are actually on, and makes it trivial
to record, in the instant, the reason a new task exists.
.PP
The tool is deliberately small in surface area and large in discipline. It has
no time tracking, no network access, no accounts, no telemetry, no background
daemon, no multi\-user features, and no natural\-language date parsing beyond a
small, documented grammar. It is one binary and one plain text file. That file
is human readable, hand editable, safe to keep in git, and safe to back up with
any tool you already trust.
.PP
The most important command is the one you type most often:
.B trk
with no arguments. It always answers the same question: what was I doing, and
why? Everything else in this manual exists to keep that answer accurate.
.SH THE NAME
The program is called
.B trk
on purpose. It is three lowercase letters, needs no shifted characters, and
becomes muscle memory within days. Typing cost is a first\-class design
constraint, and it applies to every command:
.IP \(bu 2
Commands are short, lowercase, plain words that read like thoughts
.RB ( need ,
.BR then ,
.BR by ,
.BR done ).
.IP \(bu 2
No command requires quoting. Free text is taken from the remaining arguments as
typed.
.IP \(bu 2
No command requires a shifted symbol or an awkward key combination.
.IP \(bu 2
The most common action takes the fewest keystrokes.
.IP \(bu 2
A bare
.B trk
always answers "what was I doing, and why?".
.SH CONCEPTS
Before any command makes sense, five words have to mean exactly one thing each.
They are used consistently throughout this manual.
.TP
.B Goal
A named "big thing" you are working toward. A goal has a title, a status (open
or done), and its own tree of tasks. Exactly one goal is active at a time. A
goal is the answer to "what am I ultimately trying to accomplish?"; it is not a
task and you do not "do" it directly, you finish its tasks and then close it.
.TP
.B Task
A single line of text with a state, a creation time, optional note lines,
optional stop reason, optional schedule, and an ordered list of child tasks.
A task is the answer to "what is the next concrete thing?". Tasks nest.
.TP
.B Tree
The tasks of a goal. The roots are the top\-level tasks, in order. Every task
has an ordered list of children. Order is insertion order unless a command says
otherwise. Because tasks own their children, the structure is a tree and never
a graph; a task has exactly one parent or is a root.
.TP
.B Cursor
The pointer to the current task. Each goal stores its own cursor. The cursor is
either a task id or empty. The cursor never points at a done or dropped task.
.TP
.B "Current task"
The task the cursor points at in the active goal. This is "what I am doing
right now". Many commands act on it by default.
.TP
.B State
One of open, done, dropped, stopped. Stopped means "left unfinished on purpose,
with a reason". Open and stopped tasks are both unfinished, which matters for
blocking and for numbering.
.TP
.B "Open descendant"
A task below some task that is open or stopped. "Has open children" in this
manual means "has at least one open descendant", at any depth.
.TP
.B Note
Free text attached to a task. Zero or more lines. The "why" captured when a
task is created is the first note line. It is one field, called
.B note
on the command line and shown as
.B why
in prompts and chain views.
.TP
.B Inbox
A goal\-less list of quick captures. Items are not tasks until you take them
into a goal. The inbox is for the thought you cannot afford to lose while your
hands are busy with something else.
.TP
.B Store
The flat text file holding all goals, tasks, and the inbox. See the
.B FILES
section for its location and the
.B FILE FORMAT
section for its grammar.
.TP
.B Config
The small TOML file that says where the store lives and a few display options.
See the
.B CONFIGURATION
section.
.SH HOW GOALS AND TASKS INTERACT
This is the heart of the tool. Read it once and the rest of the manual is
mostly reference.
.PP
A goal is a container and a boundary. It has a title, a status, and its own
task tree. Only one goal is active at a time; "active" means "the goal whose
tasks you are currently looking at". Switching goals is cheap and does not
disturb the other goals, because every goal remembers its own cursor. When you
come back to a goal, you resume exactly where you left off.
.PP
Tasks live inside exactly one goal. They never move between goals. A task can
have children, and a child represents "something I need in order to finish the
parent". That single rule is what makes the why chain work: if you always add a
child when you discover a prerequisite, then at any moment the path from the
goal root down to your current task is a literal list of reasons, each one a
step on the way to the goal.
.PP
Four moves cover almost everything that happens mid\-work:
.IP "1." 3
"I need X to finish this." Create a child of the current task, and descend into
it. Use
.BR "trk need X" .
.IP "2." 3
"Also, X." Create an unrelated top\-level task; you stay where you are. Use
.BR "trk by X" .
For another task at the same level as the current one, use
.BR "trk and X" .
.IP "3." 3
"Then, once this is done, X." Wrap the current chain in a new parent; you stay.
Use
.BR "trk then X" .
.IP "4." 3
"Task T needs X." Create a child of some other task T; you stay. Use
.B "trk need \-s X"
and switch to T, or move to T first.
.PP
A fifth action, finishing, walks you back up the chain and tells you where you
are and why. Use
.BR "trk done" .
.PP
The consequence of the tree is that a parent cannot be completed while it still
has open descendants. If you try,
.B trk
refuses, lists the outstanding children, and exits with a distinctive code.
This is not bureaucracy; it is the tool reminding you that the thing you called
"done" still has loose ends. When you truly mean to finish a parent anyway, you
say so explicitly with
.BR \-\-force .
.PP
Goals are closed in a similar spirit.
.B "trk goal done"
refuses while tasks remain open unless forced. A goal is a promise you made to
yourself; the tool makes you keep it or consciously break it.
.PP
Because the cursor is per goal and never rests on a finished task,
.B trk
always has an answer to "where am I?" that is a live, unfinished task, or an
honest "nowhere, this goal is finished".
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-color auto|always|never
Force color on or off, overriding the
.B color
key in the config file. The default is
.BR auto ,
which enables color only when standard output is a terminal,
.B NO_COLOR
is unset, and
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb .
This option wins over the config file. Color is never written to the store; it
only affects terminal output.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help for
.B trk
or for the subcommand that precedes it, wrapped at 80 columns.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Two global conventions are worth internalizing:
.IP \(bu 2
Free text is collected from all remaining positional arguments and joined with
single spaces. Quotes are optional. To pass text that starts with a
.BR \- ,
put
.B \-\-
before it.
.IP \(bu 2
An unknown subcommand is a usage error and exits 2.
.B trk
never treats an unknown word as new task text.
.SH COMMANDS
Each command below is described with its behavior, every option, an example,
and the thought process that would lead you to reach for it. The examples
assume the store from the
.B WORKED SESSION
at the end of this manual.
.SS trk
With no arguments,
.B trk
prints the status view and exits 0, even when there is no goal. This is the
command you will run more than any other.
.PP
The status view shows the goal title, up to four ancestors of the current task
(outermost first, with a summary line if there are more), the current task
marked with the cursor symbol, its why line, a stopped line if it is stopped,
and up to eight lines of open descendants with a summary if there are more.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      Reproduce on staging
        > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.PP
If the active goal has no current task, it prints the goal title and
.BR "no current task" ,
followed by a suggestion: if open tasks exist,
.BR "try: trk switch" ;
otherwise
.BR "try: trk goal done or trk by <task>" .
If no goal is active, it prints
.BR "no goal yet. try: trk goal new <title>" .
On a brand\-new store it prints a short hint instead.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I have been heads\-down for twenty minutes and I have lost the thread. I type
.B trk
and immediately see the chain of reasons that led me here. I do not have to
remember anything; the tree remembers for me.
.SS trk need
.nf
trk need [\-s] [\-n] [\-w why] [\-\-no\-why] text ...
.fi
.PP
"I need this to finish what I am doing." Creates a new open task and appends it
as the last child of the target, then (normally) moves the cursor onto the new
task so you descend into it. This is the single most important command for
capturing a rabbit hole.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the parent task in a picker instead of using the current task. The
cursor does not move.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-stay
Do not move the cursor onto the new task. Useful when you are recording a
prerequisite you do not intend to start yet.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIwhy\fP
Supply the why inline and skip the interactive prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt and store nothing.
.PP
If the active goal has no current task, the new task becomes a root and becomes
the current task, whatever the other options say.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk need Reproduce on staging \-w "need a failing case"
Ship login fix
  Fix login bug
    > Reproduce on staging
        why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I am fixing a login bug and realize I cannot fix it until I can see it fail. I
type
.B trk need Reproduce on staging
and answer the why prompt with
.BR "need a failing case" .
Now the task I am on is "Reproduce on staging", and the reason it exists is
recorded above it. If I get distracted, the chain still makes sense.
.SS trk by
.nf
trk by [\-w why] [\-\-no\-why] text ...
.fi
.PP
"Also, this other thing." Creates a new open task and appends it as the last
root of the active goal. The cursor does not move, unless it was empty, in which
case the new task becomes current. Prints
.B added: <text>
followed by the status view.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIwhy\fP
Supply the why inline.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk by Call internet company \-w "wifi flaky"
added: Call internet company
Ship login fix
  ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
Mid\-debug, the wifi drops and I realize I have to call the internet company.
It is not part of fixing the login bug; it is a separate top\-level task. I type
.B trk by
so it lands at the top level and I stay exactly where I was, on the login bug.
If I had typed
.BR need ,
I would have buried it under the current task, which would be wrong.
.SS trk then
.nf
trk then [\-s] [\-w why] [\-\-no\-why] text ...
.fi
.PP
"Once I am done with this, then that." Wraps the current task's whole chain in
a new task: the new task becomes the parent of the outermost ancestor (the root
of the branch) of the target, so the target and everything above it move under
the new task. The cursor does not move, unless it was empty.
.PP
This is how you build a plan outward from the first step. Each
.B then
adds the next thing to do as the new outer task. Because the new task is the
parent, it cannot be completed until the chain beneath it is done; finishing the
current task walks you up the chain in order.
.PP
For example, if you are on "Download the USB image" and run
.B trk then "Install the USB image to a flash drive"
then "Install the USB image to a flash drive" becomes the parent and "Download
the USB image" its child. Run
.B trk then "Install the image to a system"
next, and it wraps the chain again, becoming the parent of "Install the USB
image to a flash drive". Continue and you build a step\-by\-step list that
reads from the top down while you stay on the first step; finishing that step
with
.B trk done
moves you to the next one.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the target in a picker instead of using the current task.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIwhy\fP
Supply the why inline.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk then Document the process \-w "after the fix ships"
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      Reproduce on staging
        > Get staging creds
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I am about to go get staging credentials, but I realize that after the fix
ships I will need to document the process, and that step logically contains the
credential\-getting. I use
.B trk then
so "Document the process" wraps the whole chain and my current work nests inside
it. I have not lost my place; I have just given it a larger frame.
.SS trk and
.nf
trk and [\-s] [\-w why] [\-\-no\-why] text ...
.fi
.PP
Creates a new task as a sibling of the target, placed immediately after it. The
cursor does not move, unless it was empty. This is the "one more thing at the
same level" command.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the target in a picker.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIwhy\fP
Supply the why inline.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I have three errands at the same level and I have already captured the first.
Rather than start a new branch with
.BR by ,
I put the next one right beside its sibling with
.BR and .
The ordering keeps related tasks together.
.SS trk done
.nf
trk done [\-f]
.fi
.PP
Finish the current task and walk back up the chain. Marks the task done with a
completion timestamp, clears any stop reason, and moves the cursor to the next
sensible unfinished task.
.PP
The cursor movement is worth learning because it is what makes
.B trk
feel like it understands your work:
.IP \(bu 2
Let P be the nearest ancestor that is still unfinished. If P has other open
descendants, the cursor moves to the first open leaf inside P and prints a
.B next:
message naming the leaf and the task it is still needed for.
.IP \(bu 2
If P has no other open descendants, the cursor moves to P and prints a
.B back to:
message.
.IP \(bu 2
If there is no such ancestor, the cursor moves to the next unfinished top\-level
task (wrapping around) with a
.B "next top\-level task:"
message, or becomes empty if none remain.
.IP \(bu 2
If the cursor ends empty and the goal has no open tasks,
.B trk
prints a hint to close the goal.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Finish even if the task has open descendants. With
.BR \-\-force ,
the cursor jumps to the first open leaf under the finished task.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-force ,
a task with open descendants is blocked: nothing changes, an orange warning
lists the outstanding sub\-tasks, and the exit code is 3.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk done
back to: Reproduce on staging
  why: need a failing case
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      > Reproduce on staging
        why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I finally have the credentials. The thing I was on is finished, but it was a
child of "Document the process", and that parent still has work. So
.B trk done
does not dump me at the top; it walks me up exactly one level, to the smallest
unfinished thing that still needs me, and reminds me why it exists. I never have
to ask "what was I doing?" after finishing something.
.SS trk drop
.nf
trk drop [\-f] [\-w why]
.fi
.PP
Like
.BR done ,
but marks the task dropped rather than done, and follows the same cursor rules.
Dropping is deliberate and reversible with
.BR "trk undo" ;
it means "I am consciously not doing this".
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Drop even if the task has open descendants. With
.BR \-\-force ,
the entire subtree is dropped, and the cursor moves using the ancestor rules
(there is no "first open leaf under the dropped task" case).
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIwhy\fP
Append the text to the dropped task's note as
.BR "dropped: <text>" .
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I decided not to pursue this after all, but I want the record to show that the
decision was mine and why. I use
.B trk drop \-w "out of scope for this release"
so the task disappears from my working set without erasing the fact that it
existed.
.SS trk stop
.nf
trk stop [\-s] reason ...
.fi
.PP
Leave the target task unfinished, with a reason. Sets the state to stopped. The
cursor does not move. If no reason is given and a terminal is available,
.B trk
asks
.BR "why stopping? " .
A stopped task counts as unfinished everywhere; it still blocks its parent, and
it still appears in lists and pickers. The stop reason shows in the status view
as
.B "⏸ stopped: <reason>"
until the cursor lands on the task again, or
.B trk go
is run, or the task is done or dropped.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the task in a picker.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I am blocked on the registrar and cannot proceed, but the task is not done. I
.B trk stop
it with a reason so that later, when I see it, I understand why it is sitting
there. This is the difference between "unfinished and forgotten" and "unfinished
on purpose".
.SS trk go
.nf
trk go
.fi
.PP
"I am back." If the current task is stopped, print
.BR "stopped earlier: <reason>" ,
set it open, and clear the reason. Always print the why chain. It is read\-only
when the task was not stopped, and exits 4 if there is no current task.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
The registrar got back to me. I run
.B trk go
and immediately get both the acknowledgment that I had paused this and the full
chain of reasons above me, so I can resume without reconstructing context.
.SS trk why
.nf
trk why [\-s]
.fi
.PP
Print the why chain: the path from the goal root to the current task, each node
followed by its note. Nodes without a note show
.B "why: (none)"
in dim text. The target is marked with the cursor symbol. Read\-only.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Show the chain for a chosen task instead of the current task.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk why
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    why: after the fix ships
    Fix login bug
      why: customers locked out
      Reproduce on staging
        why: need a failing case
        > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
Someone asks me why I am messing with staging credentials. I run
.B trk why
and read the chain aloud: because we need a failing case, because customers are
locked out, because we are shipping the login fix. The tool answers the
question I could not have answered from memory.
.SS trk note
.nf
trk note [\-s] [\-r text] [\-c] text ...
.fi
.PP
Read or add note lines on a task. With no text, print the current task's note
lines. With text, append a new note line. Notes are shown dim in views and are
the same field as the why.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-replace \fItext\fP
Replace all note lines with the given text.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-clear
Remove all note lines.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Act on a chosen task.
.PP
After a mutation,
.B trk
prints
.B noted.
and the status view.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
The reason changed. The original why was "need a failing case", but now I know
the real problem is a stale token. I run
.B trk note \-r "stale session token, not a missing case"
so the chain tells the truth the next time I read it.
.SS trk rename
.nf
trk rename [\-s] text ...
.fi
.PP
Replace the target's text. Output is the status view.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the task in a picker.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I wrote "Fix login bug" before I understood it, and now I know it is really
"Fix session refresh". I
.B trk rename
so the label matches reality.
.SS trk switch
.nf
trk switch [N]
.fi
.PP
Choose the current task. With
.BR N ,
select row N of the numbering shown by
.BR "trk list"
(open and stopped tasks only, depth first). Without
.BR N ,
open the picker on the active goal.
.PP
After switching, the cursor moves and the landing rule applies: if the chosen
task was stopped, it is reopened and
.B trk
prints
.BR "stopped earlier: <reason>" .
If the chosen task has open descendants,
.B trk
prints an orange warning that it cannot be completed until they are done, but
the cursor still moves. Pressing Esc in the picker exits 5 and changes nothing.
Switching to the current task is a no\-op that still prints status.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk switch 5
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      > Check auth logs
        why: might explain the 401s
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I have been heads\-down on one branch and I want to deliberately switch to
another task in the same goal. I run
.B trk list
to see the numbers, then
.B trk switch 5
to jump straight there, or
.B trk switch
to choose interactively.
.SS trk list
.nf
trk list [\-a] [\-\-all\-goals]
.fi
.PP
Print the whole task tree of the active goal. The first line is the goal title.
Open and stopped tasks are numbered depth first from 1, and those numbers are
what
.B "trk switch N"
accepts. Indentation is two spaces per level, visually capped at twelve levels.
Read\-only.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Also show done and dropped tasks. They display
.B x
or
.B \-
instead of a number, are dim, and never count in the numbering. A done task
that still has open descendants is always shown, dim, as context, even without
.BR \-a .
.TP
.B \-\-all\-goals
Print every goal, with its title as a heading and a blank line between goals.
Numbering restarts per goal. Note that
.B "switch N"
only applies to the active goal.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk list
Ship login fix
 1  Document the process
 2    Fix login bug
 3      Reproduce on staging
 4        > Get staging creds
 5      Check auth logs
 6  Call internet company
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I want the shape of the whole job, not just my current branch. I run
.B trk list
to see everything, and the numbers give me a fast way to jump around.
.SS trk undo
.nf
trk undo [N]
.fi
.PP
Restore the Nth newest snapshot from the undo ring (default 1), discarding the
N\-1 newer ones and the current state. Prints
.B "undid: <label>"
followed by the status view. If the ring is empty it prints
.B "nothing to undo"
and exits 0. If N is larger than the ring it exits 1.
.PP
Undo restores a snapshot literally; it does not apply the landing rule. Undo is
not itself undoable, so there is no redo. Snapshots are whole\-file copies taken
before every successful mutation and kept up to
.B undo_depth
files deep.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I dropped the wrong task, or I renamed something badly. I type
.B trk undo
and the previous state returns. If I made several bad moves in a row, I can
.B "trk undo 3"
to step back further.
.SS trk goal
.nf
trk goal [show|list|new|switch|done|rename|reopen]
.fi
.PP
Manage goals. With no subcommand, behaves like
.BR "goal show" .
Every subcommand except
.B show
and
.B list
mutates the store.
.SS "trk goal show"
Print the active goal's title, id, state, creation time, and counts of open and
done tasks. Read\-only.
.SS "trk goal list"
Print a numbered list of goals, the active one marked with the cursor symbol,
with open task counts and
.B (done)
for completed goals. The numbers are what
.B "goal switch N"
accepts.
.SS "trk goal new"
.nf
trk goal new [\-n] title ...
.fi
Create an open goal with an empty tree and make it active, unless
.B \-n/\-\-stay
is given. This is how a session usually begins.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk goal new Ship login fix
goal: Ship login fix
Ship login fix
no current task
try: trk goal done or trk by <task>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I am starting something new and large. Before I can track steps I need to name
the thing I am aiming at. I run
.B trk goal new
with the outcome, not the first step.
.SS "trk goal switch"
.nf
trk goal switch [N|text]
.fi
Make another open goal active. With no argument, open a picker of open goals.
.B N
is the number from
.BR "goal list" .
Text is a case\-insensitive substring; exactly one match is required, otherwise
.B trk
exits 1 listing the matches. Output is the status view for the new active goal.
Each goal keeps its own cursor.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
The login fix can wait; the release is on fire. I run
.B trk goal switch release
and I am instantly back inside that goal at the exact task I left current.
.SS "trk goal done"
.nf
trk goal done [\-f]
.fi
Mark the active goal done. If it has open tasks and
.B \-\-force
is not given, exit 3 and list up to five of them. With
.BR \-\-force ,
the tasks stay as they are. Afterwards the next open goal in file order
(wrapping) becomes active, or none does. Prints
.B "goal done: <title>"
and the new status view.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
Every task under "Ship login fix" is finished, so the goal itself is finished. I
run
.B trk goal done
to close the promise. If I try it too early,
.B trk
stops me and shows what is left.
.SS "trk goal rename"
.nf
trk goal rename title ...
.fi
Rename the active goal.
.SS "trk goal reopen"
.nf
trk goal reopen [N|text]
.fi
Reopen a done goal (picker if no argument) and make it active. Matching is the
same as
.BR "goal switch" .
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
We shipped, but a follow\-up ticket means the goal is alive again. Rather than
make a new goal, I
.B trk goal reopen
the old one and its history comes with it.
.SS trk jot
.nf
trk jot text ...
.fi
Append an item to the inbox. There is no why prompt. It never touches any goal
or cursor, and it works with no goals at all. Prints
.BR "jotted: <text>  (inbox: N)" .
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I am in the middle of something and a stray thought arrives: "look into sqlite
wal mode". I do not want to file it, decide what it belongs to, or break my
flow. I type
.B trk jot look into wal mode
and the thought is safe.
.SS trk inbox
.nf
trk inbox
.fi
List the inbox items, numbered oldest first, with their creation date.
Read\-only. When empty it prints
.BR "inbox is empty" .
.SS "trk inbox take"
.nf
trk inbox take [N] [\-\-goal name | \-\-new\-goal title] [\-s] [\-a]
.fi
Take an item out of the inbox and turn it into a task.
.IP "1." 3
Choose the item:
.BR N ,
or a picker if omitted. If the inbox has exactly one item, it is used
automatically.
.IP "2." 3
Choose the goal:
.B \-\-goal
(matching as in
.BR "goal switch" ),
.B \-\-new\-goal
(create it), or interactively a picker of open goals.
.IP "3." 3
Create a task from the item text in that goal, as a root, appended last.
.IP "4." 3
Ask for a why, then remove the item from the inbox.
.IP "5." 3
The active goal and cursors are unchanged unless
.B \-a/\-\-activate
is given, which makes the goal active. The empty\-cursor rule applies inside
that goal.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
After the goal is chosen, switch to the parent task instead of making a root.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-activate
Make the chosen goal active.
.PP
Non\-interactive use requires
.B N
and one of
.B \-\-goal
or
.BR \-\-new\-goal .
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
Later in the day I review my jots. "look into wal mode" actually belongs to the
performance goal, so I run
.B trk inbox take 1 \-\-goal performance
and it becomes a real, prioritized task with a recorded why.
.SS "trk inbox drop"
.nf
trk inbox drop [N]
.fi
Remove an item from the inbox (picker if no
.BR N ).
Prints
.BR "dropped: <text>" .
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
The jot turned out to be irrelevant. I
.B trk inbox drop 1
and it is gone, without ever having polluted a goal.
.SS trk log
.nf
trk log [when]
.fi
Print what got finished, by day, newest first. The header of each day shows the
weekday, date, and counts of tasks done and added. Done tasks are sorted by
completion time, with the goal name in brackets right\-aligned when it fits and
on its own indented line when it does not. Notes are shown dim, first line
only. Days with nothing done print
.BR "nothing finished" .
.PP
.B when
is
.B today
(the default),
.BR yesterday ,
an integer
.B N
(the last N days including today), or
.BR YYYY\-MM\-DD .
Read\-only.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk log
Wed 2026\-10\-07   4 done, 6 added
  09:40  Write repro steps                  [Ship login fix]
         why: need a failing case
  10:15  Get staging creds                  [Ship login fix]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
At the end of the week I want evidence of progress, not a vague sense of it. I
run
.B trk log 7
and see what actually shipped.
.SS trk at
.nf
trk at [\-s] [\-\-clear] when ...
.fi
Set the target's schedule. The schedule is stored as an
.B @ at=
attribute and surfaces in
.BR "trk agenda" .
.TP
.B \-\-clear
Remove the schedule.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the task in a picker.
.PP
With no
.B when
and a terminal,
.B trk
asks two questions:
.BR "date (YYYY\-MM\-DD, today, tomorrow, mon..sun, +Nd): "
then
.BR "time (HH:MM, Enter for none): " .
.PP
The grammar for
.B when
is a date token followed by an optional time token:
.IP \(bu 2
Date:
.BR today ,
.BR tomorrow ,
.B mon
through
.B sun
(the next such day, not today),
.BR YYYY\-MM\-DD ,
or
.B +Nd
(N days from now).
.IP \(bu 2
Time:
.B HH:MM
(24 hour) or
.B H
/
.B HH
followed by
.B am
or
.BR pm .
.IP \(bu 2
A time alone (
.BR 15:00 ,
.BR 3pm )
means today if still in the future, otherwise tomorrow.
.PP
Anything else exits 2 with the grammar in the message.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk at tomorrow 3pm
Ship login fix
  > Call internet company
    why: wifi flaky
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
Calling the internet company is not urgent today but must not slip. I run
.B trk at tomorrow 3pm
so it appears on the agenda and stops living only in my head.
.SS trk agenda
.nf
trk agenda
.fi
Print scheduled tasks across all goals. Sections are Overdue, then each date in
ascending order. Within a date, timed tasks come first by time, then untimed
tasks. Only open and stopped tasks with an
.B at
attribute appear. Past dates with no time count as overdue starting the next
day. Read\-only.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Overdue
  Mon 2026\-10\-05        Renew domain                   [Sticker shop]
Today  Wed 2026\-10\-07
  15:00                 Call internet company          [Ship login fix]
Fri 2026\-10\-09
  (no time)             Fix dns record                 [Ship login fix]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
It is the start of the day and I want to know what is due, across every goal at
once. I run
.B trk agenda
and let the tool hold the calendar.
.SS trk start
.nf
trk start [\-\-focus]
.fi
Open the live, full\-screen interface meant to sit in a small tmux or wezterm
pane beside your work. It works in any terminal that supports ANSI escapes and
does not depend on a particular shell or OS.
.TP
.B \-\-focus
Start in focus view: the goal, up to four ancestors of the highlighted task,
the task, and its open descendants.
.PP
The layout is a title bar with the active goal and open count, a task pane, a
why pane for the highlighted task, a transient message line, and a footer of key
hints. The keys are:
.IP \(bu 2
.B j
/
.B Down
and
.B k
/
.B Up
move the highlight (which does not change the current task).
.IP \(bu 2
.B PgUp
/
.B PgDn
page;
.B c
jumps the highlight back to the current task.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Enter
picks the highlighted task, exactly as
.BR "trk switch" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B d
completes it as
.BR "trk done" ;
.B s
stops it and asks for a reason;
.B g
runs
.BR "trk go" ;
.B n
adds a note.
.IP \(bu 2
.B u
undoes;
.B :
opens a command line that accepts the same commands as the CLI (except
.BR start ,
.B config
and
.BR completions );
.B Tab
toggles full and focus views;
.B w
toggles a full\-height why view;
.B r
force\-reloads;
.B ?
shows help; and
.B q
or Ctrl\-C quits.
.PP
The view polls the store and reloads automatically when another terminal
changes it, so running
.B trk need
in your main pane is reflected in the pane running
.B trk start
within about half a second. The terminal is restored on every exit path,
including after a panic.
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I keep
.B trk start
open in a narrow pane all day. My main terminal is where I actually work; the
pane shows me the tree, the why chain, and the current task without my having to
ask. When I want to change something from the keyboard I press
.B :
and type a normal command.
.SS trk config
.nf
trk config [show|path]
.fi
With no subcommand, run the interactive configuration flow (see
.BR "FIRST RUN" ).
.B "config show"
prints the effective values after environment overrides, the config file path,
the store path, and the backups folder.
.B "config path"
prints only the config file path.
.PP
With non\-interactive standard input,
.B trk config
exits 1 with a message telling you to create the file by hand or set
.BR TRK_STORE .
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I want to move my task list into a synced folder, or onto another disk. I run
.B trk config
and answer two questions; the tool writes the config, offers to move the
existing store and backups, and prints the new paths.
.SS trk prompt
.nf
trk prompt [\-\-max N]
.fi
For embedding in a shell prompt. Prints the current task text, truncated to
.B \-\-max
columns (default 30) with an ellipsis, and no trailing newline. Prints nothing
if there is no current task.
.B trk prompt
never prompts, never takes the lock, never prints errors, and always exits 0,
even on failure; its target is under ten milliseconds.
.TP
.B \-\-max \fIN\fP
Maximum width in columns before truncation.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# bash
PS1='$(trk prompt) '"$PS1"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I want the current task visible without running anything. I put
.B trk prompt
in my shell prompt and every new prompt quietly reminds me what I am on.
.SS trk completions
.nf
trk completions shell
.fi
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.B powershell ,
or
.BR elvish .
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk completions bash  > ~/.local/share/bash\-completion/completions/trk
$ trk completions zsh   > "${fpath[1]}/_trk"
$ trk completions fish  > ~/.config/fish/completions/trk.fish
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Thought process:"
I have internalized the commands, but tab completion still saves keystrokes and
prevents typos. I install the script once.
.SH FIRST RUN
The first time you run a command that needs the store,
.B trk
checks for a config file. If there is none and
.B TRK_STORE
is unset, it runs the configuration flow before continuing. The exceptions are
.BR help ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-V ,
.B completions
and
.BR config .
If there is no terminal, it exits 1 and tells you how to proceed by hand.
.PP
The configuration flow asks two questions:
.RS 4
.nf
Where should trk keep your task list?
(a plain text file; you can back it up or put it in git)
path [~/.local/share/trk/tasks.trk]:

Where should trk keep its backups of the task list?
(undo snapshots; a folder, ideally somewhere you also back up)
folder [~/.local/share/trk/backups]:
.fi
.RE
.PP
Press Enter to accept each default. The store may start with
.B ~
or
.BR $HOME ,
and is expanded on load. If the store already exists and parses, the flow says
how many goals it found and uses it. If it does not exist, the parent
directories are created and an empty, valid store is written. The backups
folder must be a directory; it is created if missing and may live on another
disk or inside a synced folder. If a previous config pointed elsewhere and those
files exist, you are offered the chance to move them.
.PP
The very first time you create a task in an empty store, a short walkthrough
runs: it prints a few lines of introduction, asks
.BR "What *are* you working on?" ,
creates a goal from your answer, and then runs the original command against it.
The walkthrough runs once, ever; it only triggers when there are no goals. A
bare
.B trk
on an empty store does not run it; it prints a hint instead.
.SH A WORKED SESSION
This is a complete, realistic session from an empty store to a finished goal,
with the thought process at each step. It matches the acceptance scenario used
to test the program, so you can reproduce it exactly.
.PP
.I "Step 0: starting a goal."
I am about to fix a login problem. Before I touch the code I name the outcome.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk goal new Ship login fix
goal: Ship login fix
Ship login fix
no current task
try: trk goal done or trk by <task>
.fi
.RE
I now have an empty goal and no current task. The tool is telling me the next
move is to add a task.
.PP
.I "Step 1: the first task."
The first concrete thing is the bug itself. It is top level, so I use
.BR by ,
not
.BR need .
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk by Fix login bug \-w "customers locked out"
added: Fix login bug
Ship login fix
  > Fix login bug
      why: customers locked out
.fi
.RE
The empty\-cursor rule made this task current even though
.B by
normally stays put.
.PP
.I "Step 2: a prerequisite."
I cannot fix what I cannot see, so I descend.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk need Reproduce on staging \-w "need a failing case"
Ship login fix
  Fix login bug
    > Reproduce on staging
        why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
I am now two levels deep and the chain reads like a sentence: I am reproducing
on staging because I need a failing case, to fix the login bug, because
customers are locked out.
.PP
.I "Step 3: another prerequisite."
Reproducing needs access.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk need Get staging creds \-w "can't log in"
Ship login fix
  Fix login bug
    Reproduce on staging
      > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Step 4: an unrelated interruption."
The wifi drops. This is not part of the login fix; it is a sibling at the top.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk by Call internet company \-w "wifi flaky"
added: Call internet company
Ship login fix
  Fix login bug
    Reproduce on staging
      > Get staging creds
.fi
.RE
Notice my cursor did not move. The interruption was captured without derailing
me.
.PP
.I "Step 5: a task that belongs to an earlier one."
I realize I should also check the auth logs, and that belongs to "Fix login
bug", not to my current task. I switch to the target.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk switch 1
Ship login fix
  > Fix login bug
      why: customers locked out
$ trk need \-n Check auth logs \-w "might explain the 401s"
$ trk switch 3
.fi
.RE
I used
.B \-n
so adding the child did not drag me down into it, then moved back to where I
was.
.PP
.I "Step 6: reframing the work."
I realize the credential step is contained by a documentation step that happens
after shipping.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk then Document the process \-w "after the fix ships"
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      Reproduce on staging
        > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.B then
wrapped my whole chain in the new task. My place is unchanged, but it now sits
inside a larger, correct frame.
.PP
.I "Step 7: finishing the leaf."
I have the credentials.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk done
back to: Reproduce on staging
  why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
.B trk
walked me up exactly one level, to the nearest unfinished ancestor, and reminded
me why it exists. If instead a sibling had still needed me, it would have said
.B next:
and named it.
.PP
.I "Step 8: climbing the chain."
I continue finishing, one sensible step at a time:
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk done    # Reproduce on staging is done; next: Check auth logs
$ trk done    # Check auth logs is done; back to Fix login bug
$ trk done    # Fix login bug is done; back to Document the process
$ trk done    # Document the process is done; next top\-level task: Call internet company
$ trk done    # Call internet company is done; goal has no open tasks
.fi
.RE
At each step the message told me exactly where I landed and why. When the goal
ran out of work,
.B trk
printed
.BR "goal has no open tasks. when you are done: trk goal done" .
.PP
.I "Step 9: undoing a mistake."
I finished the last task by accident and want it back.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk undo
undid: done
Ship login fix
  > Call internet company
    why: wifi flaky
.fi
.RE
The task is open again and current. There is no redo; if I had wanted to step
back further I would have run
.BR "trk undo 2" .
.PP
.I "Step 10: closing the goal."
When I mean it, I close the goal.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk goal done
goal done: Ship login fix
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Step 11: the stray thought."
At some point during all this, a thought arrived that did not belong anywhere
yet.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk jot look into sqlite wal mode
jotted: look into sqlite wal mode  (inbox: 1)
.fi
.RE
Later, when I had a spare minute, I filed it:
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk inbox take 1 \-\-goal "Ship login fix"
.fi
.RE
.SH FILE FORMAT
The store is one flat UTF\-8 text file, line oriented, written with LF and read
with CRLF tolerated. It is rewritten whole and atomically on every change; it is
not append\-only. It is never wrapped; wrapping applies only to terminal output.
.PP
A store has a header, an optional inbox, and zero or more goal blocks. Here is
a complete example:
.RS 4
.nf
trk 1
next g=2 t=7 i=1
active g1

inbox
  i3 2026\-10\-07T09:30:00\-05:00 look into sqlite wal mode

goal g1 open 2026\-10\-07T09:00:00\-05:00 Ship login fix
  current t3
  [ ] t6 2026\-10\-07T09:06:00\-05:00 Document the process
    | after the fix ships
    [ ] t1 2026\-10\-07T09:01:00\-05:00 Fix login bug
      | customers locked out
      [ ] t2 2026\-10\-07T09:02:00\-05:00 Reproduce on staging
        | need a failing case
        [ ] t3 2026\-10\-07T09:03:00\-05:00 Get staging creds
          | can't log in
      [ ] t5 2026\-10\-07T09:05:00\-05:00 Check auth logs
        | might explain the 401s
  [ ] t4 2026\-10\-07T09:04:00\-05:00 Call internet company
    | wifi flaky
.fi
.RE
.PP
The header is
.B trk
followed by the format version, then a
.B next
line with the next unused counter per kind, then an optional
.B active
line naming the active goal. Comments begin with
.B #
at column zero and are ignored and dropped on the next write.
.PP
Inside a goal, the
.B current
line names the cursor. Task lines are indented two spaces per level and begin
with a bracketed state:
.B [ ]
open,
.B [x]
done,
.B [-]
dropped,
.B [!]
stopped. A done or dropped task must carry
.B @ done=
or
.B @ dropped=
with an RFC 3339 timestamp. A scheduled task carries
.B @ at=
with either a timestamp or a date. A stopped task must carry a
.B !
reason line. Notes are
.B |
lines. Indentation is exactly two spaces per level; tabs are a parse error.
Unknown line types or bad structure produce a parse error with a line number;
.B trk
never guesses and never partially loads.
.PP
Parsing then serializing a valid file produces canonical text, so hand edits are
allowed and normalized on the next write.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config file is TOML. Its location, in order, is:
.BR $TRK_CONFIG ,
then
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/trk/config.toml ,
then
.BR $HOME/.config/trk/config.toml .
On Windows
.B $HOME
means the user profile directory; the path is otherwise the same everywhere.
.PP
The keys are:
.TP
.B store
Required. Path to the store. May start with
.B ~
or
.BR $HOME .
.TP
.B backups
Directory for undo snapshots. Optional; defaults to a
.B backups
directory next to the store.
.TP
.B color
.BR auto | always | never .
Default
.BR auto .
.TP
.B wrap
Maximum output width in columns, clamped to the range 40 to 200. Default 80.
.TP
.B undo_depth
Number of undo snapshots kept. Default 50.
.TP
.B prompt_why
Ask
.B why?
when creating a task. Default true.
.TP
.B ascii
.BR auto | always | never .
Default
.BR auto .
.TP
.B poll_ms
Change\-detection interval for
.BR "trk start" ,
in milliseconds. Default 500.
.PP
Unknown keys produce a warning, not an error. Invalid values exit 1 naming the
key and the file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRK_CONFIG
Path to the config file. Overrides the default location.
.TP
.B TRK_STORE
Path to the store. Overrides the
.B store
key in the config. If set and no config exists, the first\-run flow is skipped.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables color.
.TP
.B TRK_ASCII
Set to
.B 1
to force ASCII symbols.
.TP
.B TRK_NOW
Undocumented; for tests only. An RFC 3339 time used as "now".
.SH OUTPUT AND SYMBOLS
Normal output goes to standard output; warnings and errors go to standard
error. Wrapping width is the smaller of
.B wrap
and the terminal width, and wrapping is display\-width aware, preserving hanging
indents.
.PP
Errors look like
.BR "trk: <lowercase message>" .
Warnings look like
.B "warning: <message>"
and are printed in orange. Orange is ANSI 256\-color 208; terminals without 256
colors get yellow. Color is on only when standard output is a terminal,
.B NO_COLOR
is unset, and
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb ;
.B \-\-color
and
.B config.color
can force it.
.PP
Symbols have an ASCII fallback, used when the locale is not UTF\-8, or on
Windows, or when
.B TRK_ASCII=1
or
.B ascii = "always"
is set:
.RS 4
.nf
Meaning          Unicode   ASCII
cursor             ►        >
stopped marker     ⏸        !
up/back arrow      ↑        ^
ellipsis           …     ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
The cursor row is bold; done and dropped rows are dim; notes are dim; warnings
are orange; errors are red.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error: I/O, parse failure, lock timeout, or an invalid value.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including unknown subcommands and bad
.B at
grammar.
.TP
.B 3
Blocked: a task has open children and
.B \-\-force
was not given, or a goal has open tasks and
.B \-\-force
was not given.
.TP
.B 4
Nothing to act on: no active goal, or no current task.
.TP
.B 5
Cancelled by the user in a picker or prompt.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/trk/config.toml
Default config file (or under
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/trk/tasks.trk
Default store (or under
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME ).
.TP
.I <store>.lock
Advisory lock held for the duration of a mutation; created on demand.
.TP
.I <backups>/NNNNNN.trk
Undo snapshots, each prefixed with a
.B "# undo\-label:"
comment recording the command that caused it.
.SH NOTES
.B trk
has no time tracking, no network access, sync, accounts, or telemetry, no
background daemon, no multi\-user features, no redo, and no natural\-language
date parsing beyond the grammar in
.BR "trk at" .
Future ideas, deliberately not in this version, include reordering and
reparenting, search, export, tags, repeating tasks, a calendar picker, theming,
and an edit command that opens
.BR $EDITOR .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR git (1)
.PP
Run
.B trk \-\-help
or
.B trk command \-\-help
for a quick reminder of any command and its options.
